title: Diagnosis of CMML
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

* ''Establish:'' Persistent peripheral blood monocytosis ≥0.5 × 10^9/L and ≥10% of leukocytes, generally documented over ≥3 months, with compatible marrow findings and <20% blasts in blood and marrow. Count promonocytes as blast equivalents.
* ''Workup:'' Serial complete blood counts/differentials, smear, marrow aspirate/biopsy, cytogenetics, and myeloid sequencing. Exclude reactive infection/inflammation, marrow recovery, BCR::ABL1-positive leukemia, and other myeloproliferative neoplasms. Especially with eosinophilia, exclude [[tyrosine-kinase fusion neoplasms|Tyrosine-kinase fusion neoplasms]] (including PDGFRA, PDGFRB, FGFR1, and JAK2 rearrangements).
* ''WHO fifth edition:'' With monocytes ≥1 × 10^9/L, require ≥1 supporting feature: dysplasia, acquired clonal cytogenetic/molecular abnormality, or abnormal monocyte partitioning. At 0.5–<1 × 10^9/L, require both dysplasia and clonality. Mutations alone do not establish CMML.
* ''ICC distinction:'' International Consensus Classification incorporates cytopenia and, for mutation-based clonality, variant allele frequency ≥10%; rare early cases lack cytopenia. Without clonality, monocytes must be ≥1 × 10^9/L with increased blasts, dysplasia, or a CMML-consistent abnormal immunophenotype. Specify the classification used.
* ''Flow adjunct:'' Classical CD14++/CD16− monocytes >94% support CMML; inflammatory disease can confound results. This does not replace marrow assessment.
* ''Subtypes:'' Myelodysplastic CMML has WBC <13 × 10^9/L; myeloproliferative CMML ≥13. CMML-1: blood blasts <5% and marrow <10%; CMML-2: blood 5–19%, marrow 10–19%, or Auer rods. CMML-0 is obsolete. Proceed to [[risk assessment|Risk stratification and transplantation in CMML]].

Sources: [[WHO 2022, Table 6|https://doi.org/10.1038/s41375-022-01613-1]]; [[ICC 2022, Table 13|https://doi.org/10.1182/blood.2022015850]]; [[Patnaik 2022|https://doi.org/10.3324/haematol.2021.279500]].
